//! `docs/plan/57-grass.md` rungs G1, G2 and G3: a field of grass, generated by
//! compute and drawn beside the opaque frame as cards, as mesh blades, as
//! shells, or as any mix of the three.
//!
//! ```text
//!               ┌──── one dispatch per tile ────┐   ┌── one draw per slot ──┐
//!  grass-clear ─┴─▶ grass-generate ─ instances ─┴──▶ grass (colour + depth)
//!        │                  ▲                            │
//!        └── draw args ─────┘                            └─▶ sky, ssr, bloom …
//! ```
//!
//! Two halves, owned in two places, for `crate::forward::view`'s line between
//! the scene and a camera — the `water` module's arrangement exactly:
//!
//! * `GrassScene` is the **scene's**: the field a caller set, its three
//!   textures, the blade table, the per-tile blocks and the buffers a dispatch
//!   writes. Every view generates and draws the same field.
//! * `Grass` is **one camera's**: the seven pipelines, its three uniform rings
//!   and the bind groups naming its own transients.
//!
//! # A field is content, not an effect
//!
//! On the `water` module's terms, which are `sky_pass`'s: a frame with no
//! field records **no pass**, takes no transient and binds nothing, so it is the
//! frame this renderer drew before this module existed, bit for bit. There is no
//! [`RenderEffects`](crate::RenderEffects) bit to turn grass off; removing the
//! field is what does.
//!
//! # Where the three passes go, and why
//!
//! **After the forward pass and before the background.** Grass is opaque scene
//! content with a cutout, so the depth it writes has to be in the buffer before
//! the sky decides which pixels it covers, before the Hi-Z pyramid is built and
//! before the reflection march reads the frame. It attaches the scene colour and
//! the scene depth in place, so every pass below reads the same two images it
//! always did.
//!
//! **It is not in the depth prepass.** Horizon Zero Dawn's arrangement —
//! decision 1's "depth-only prepass then an equal-depth pass: zero percent
//! overdraw" — is an overdraw optimisation, and what it saves is proportional to
//! how much grass covers a pixel. `docs/backlog.md` carries it with the density
//! at which it starts paying.
//!
//! # Three passes, not two
//!
//! The appends are an atomic add on the very word that holds a slot's instance
//! count, and the graph emits its barriers between passes rather than inside
//! one — so the clear that zeroes those words is a pass of its own.
//! `draw_gen.slang`'s `clear-counters` is the same arrangement for the same
//! reason.
//!
//! # Three looks, one pass
//!
//! The `grass` pass draws a slot's cards, then its shells, then its fins, then
//! its near and its far mesh blades, each through its own pipeline over one bind
//! group and each from its own fixed slot of the argument buffer — see
//! `crcbl_shaders::grass::DRAWS_PER_SLOT`. A field none of whose rows is
//! [`BladeLook::Shells`] records no shell or fin draw at all, and one none of
//! whose rows is [`BladeLook::Blades`] no blade draw, so a card field's command
//! stream is the one rung G1 recorded.
//!
//! The two blade draws are decision 3's two levels of detail, fifteen vertices
//! and seven, and the generation pass decides which a blade is in by its root's
//! distance from the camera — see `grass_gen.slang`'s header for the runs they
//! are appended to and `grass.slang`'s for how the near level morphs into the
//! far one before the switch.
//!
//! The shells find their strands in a second buffer the generation pass writes,
//! `cells`: every cell's blade at the cell's own index. It is written for every
//! look, so the placement a card field and a shell field of one description
//! carry is one buffer's bytes — `tests/render_e2e/grass_shells.rs` compares
//! them.
//!
//! # A slot's run, and `firstInstance`
//!
//! WebGPU requires an indirect draw's `firstInstance` to be zero unless
//! `indirect-first-instance` is enabled, so a slot's run of the shared instance
//! buffer is reached by the base the vertex stage adds — and the tile block that
//! carries the slot is bound at a **dynamic offset**, one row of a ring per
//! tile. That is decision 1's "each slot binds its own buffer offset", spent on
//! a uniform block rather than on a storage binding: the forward pass's bucket
//! constants already ride that mechanism on all four backends.

